A checker thread shares a huge file's statements with forks of itself

A checker checks a file on one thread, and one file can be most of a program's check. The
TypeScript compiler's checker.ts is one function of three megabytes: the thread that checks it
runs twice as long as the nine threads that check the 77 other files of the compiler, whose
processors then wait for it.

Where a new thread is a fork of the thread that starts it (the garnet build: a worker is a forked
process and has the starting thread's memory as it is, copy-on-write; `startThreads(..., forks)`),
a checker thread that is in a huge container, a file or a function declared at the top of one,
when another checker runs out of files starts a fork of itself for the idle processor. The fork
has every type, link and cache the checker had. The checker and its forks take the container's
remaining statements one at a time from a cursor in shared memory; a fork checks what it takes,
posts the diagnostics it added and ends, and the thread adds them to its own before it reports
the file (checker-split.ts, `ThreadSplit` in parallel-check.ts). Until a processor is idle
nothing differs from a checker that does not share.

The diagnostics are those of the checker alone (the reasons are at the top of checker-split.ts):
a statement is checked in full by one process and everything else is lazy; what was queued
before a fork stays the checker's; a deferred node belongs to the process that takes the
statement around it; a run of unreachable statements is reported with its first statement; an
interface's once-only checks run at the first of its shared declarations. A fork that fails is
replaced by the checker, which checks what the fork had taken. A container is kept whole where
every statement's side effects must be in one checker: unused identifiers, emit and declaration
diagnostics, imported helpers, and a scope that declares WeakMap, WeakSet or Reflect below
ES2022.

The TypeScript compiler project, type check only, ten checkers: 0.36 to 0.27 s (median of 11
alternating rounds on a loaded machine, without PGO). TypeScript's test cases, each compiled by a
checker alone and with forks forced at the first, second or third statement (TSC_TS_SPLIT_FORKS,
_AT, _BODY, _MIN, _FILE): 6,537 of 6,537 outputs identical, forks started in 4,904 of them, also
with a fork made to fail in each (TSC_TS_SPLIT_FAIL).

